course: dictionary
lesson: 6853
title: Weighing things up
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Picture old kitchen scales with a bag of feathers on one side and a bag of bricks on the other. The bricks are heavier, so they win. To outweigh something means to be heavier than it. We also use the word for ideas: if one thing counts for more, it outweighs the thing that counts for less.
explain: «The good points outweigh the bad.» Imagine the good points on one side of the scales and the bad points on the other. If the good side is heavier, the good points are more important. Ask yourself: which side is heavier?
explain: «Keeping someone safe outweighs a promise.» A promise matters, but keeping someone safe matters more, so safety wins. Check your meaning by swapping it in: "keeping someone safe is more important than a promise" still makes sense, so you have got it right.
fact: Outweigh is a verb, so its forms are outweigh, outweighs, outweighed and outweighing.
fact: «Outweigh» means to be heavier than something else, or to be more important or bigger in effect.
fact: «The benefits outweigh the risks» means the good results are bigger than the dangers.
fact: «The pros outweigh the cons» means the good points are more important than the bad points.
fact: «Far outweigh» means to outweigh by a lot, so one side is much more important than the other.
exercise: «The benefits of the vaccine outweigh the risks.» What does outweigh mean here? | Are the same as | *Are bigger and more important than | Are written down | Are smaller and hidden away
exercise: On a balance, a heavy brick outweighs a feather. What does outweigh mean? | *Is heavier than | Is the same colour as | Is lighter than | Is further away
exercise: «Sometimes a more important duty outweighs a rule.» Which meaning fits best? | Is hidden by | Is shorter than | *Is more important than | Is printed in
test: «A promise is a real duty: only a stronger duty can outweigh it.» Which form of the verb shows something that happened in the past? | *outweighed | outweigh | outweighs | outweighing
test: Ben says «the pros outweigh the cons». What does he mean? | *The good points are more important than the bad points | The cons are written in capital letters | The pros and cons are exactly the same size | The bad points are heavier, so he is sad
exam: Ella says «helping a hurt child outweighs a small rule». What is she saying? | Rules and children weigh exactly the same | A small rule is heavier than a hurt child | Helping a child is a rule that must never be broken | *Helping a hurt child is more important than a small rule
